To Whom It May Concern:

Pursuant to the Freedom of Information Act, I hereby request the following records:

Please note that this submission is a resubmission and replacement of my previously submitted FOIA request concerning Christopher Furnari, for which EOUSA assigned tracking number EOUSA-2026-005348. I have requested that EOUSA-2026-005348 be closed.

I am resubmitting the request because I have revised and clarified its scope and search parameters. Please treat this submission, rather than my prior submission, as the operative version of the request.

I respectfully request that EOUSA assign this resubmission a new FOIA tracking number and provide confirmation of receipt and the new tracking number.

For clarity, this submission is intended to supersede the prior request associated with EOUSA-2026-005348. Please do not treat this as an additional duplicate request seeking separate processing of the same version.

Pursuant to the Freedom of Information Act, I request all records maintained by the Executive Office for United States Attorneys (EOUSA), including records held by or associated with the United States Attorney’s Offices for the Southern District of New York, Middle District of Pennsylvania, and United States Attorney’s Office for the Eastern District of Minnesota, concerning or pertaining to Christopher Furnari’s federal parole proceedings and parole-related litigation.

Furnari’s Bureau of Prisons Register Number is:

19815-054

Date Range

This request seeks records created, received, maintained, or otherwise held from January 1, 1985 through December 31, 2019, concerning or pertaining to Furnari’s federal parole proceedings.

However, the foregoing date range does not exclude earlier-created records where such records were subsequently used, reviewed, relied upon, summarized, incorporated, transmitted, provided, or otherwise brought into connection with Furnari’s federal parole proceedings or parole-related litigation.

Accordingly, records originally created or maintained before January 1, 1985, including records originating in the 1970s or earlier, are responsive to this request where they were subsequently used or relied upon in connection with Furnari’s federal parole, including as evidence, background information, factual support, witness information, investigative material, offense-severity information, or material concerning conduct attributed to Furnari.

The relevant consideration is therefore not solely the date on which an underlying record was originally created, but whether that record was subsequently incorporated into, relied upon, maintained for, or otherwise connected to Furnari’s federal parole proceedings or litigation.

Approximately Two-Foot Collection

This request specifically includes, but is not limited to, the entirety of the approximately two-foot stack of files referenced in a United States Parole Commission Original Jurisdiction Appeal Summary concerning Furnari, Reg. No. 19815-054. I will attach a copy of this document.

The approximately two-foot collection is specifically identified because it appears to represent a substantial body of records associated with Furnari’s case.

It is not intended to limit or define the scope of this request. The principal purpose of this request is to obtain the complete body of EOUSA and United States Attorney’s Office records pertaining to Furnari’s federal parole proceedings and parole-related litigation.

If the approximately two-foot collection is maintained under a case number, matter number, file number, subject number, box number, archive identifier, or other internal identifier, please provide that identifier and any associated index, inventory, file-control record, routing sheet, transfer record, or other existing record sufficient to identify the complete collection.

If the collection was divided into multiple files, folders, boxes, volumes, electronic repositories, or other components, please treat those components collectively as responsive where they form part of the referenced collection.

If EOUSA or a United States Attorney’s Office possesses only a portion of the collection and another DOJ component, Federal Records Center, NARA facility, or other repository possesses another portion, please identify the location of the remaining material and provide any available identifying information.



Scope of Requested Parole Records

I request all records pertaining to or concerning Furnari’s federal parole proceedings, including records created before, during, or after an individual parole proceeding where those records were subsequently used, maintained, reviewed, relied upon, or incorporated in connection with Furnari’s federal parole.

This includes records concerning his parole eligibility, parole hearings, reconsideration proceedings, appeals, requests for reconsideration, release considerations, government recommendations or objections, and any other federal parole proceeding involving Furnari.

This includes all records pertaining to the following, but is not limited to:

* all records submitted to, received from, or exchanged with the United States Parole Commission;
* all records concerning the Government’s position regarding Furnari’s release;
* all recommendations, objections, memoranda, letters, reports, factual submissions, and other materials submitted in support of or opposition to parole;
* all records concerning the offense-severity category assigned to Furnari and the factual basis used to support that determination;
* all records concerning his guideline calculations, salient factor score, aggravating circumstances, or other factors considered in determining whether he should be released;
* all records concerning information used to deny, defer, oppose, or otherwise adversely affect Furnari’s parole;
* all records concerning any subsequent reconsideration or review of those decisions;
* all internal memoranda, correspondence, notes, emails, and other communications concerning how the United States Attorney’s Office responded to Furnari’s parole proceedings;
* all communications between EOUSA, the relevant United States Attorney’s Offices, the Bureau of Prisons, the United States Parole Commission, federal courts, and other federal or state agencies concerning Furnari’s parole; and
* all records concerning any federal litigation arising from or relating to Furnari’s parole.

This request also includes all records concerning the murders, attempted murders, or other criminal conduct attributed to Furnari to the extent that such matters were considered, relied upon, presented, or otherwise used in connection with his federal parole proceedings, including information concerning Lee Schleifer, Richard Taglianetti, Frank DeCicco, Joseph Abinanti, Anthony Luongo, and any other offense or incident relied upon by the Government in seeking to establish or support a Severity of Offense Behavior Category Eight classification.


Underlying Evidence and Investigative Material Used in Furnari’s Parole Proceedings

This request also includes underlying investigative, prosecutorial, evidentiary, witness, informant, or other records to the extent that those records were used, obtained, reviewed, relied upon, summarized, incorporated, transmitted, provided, or otherwise brought into Furnari’s federal parole proceedings or parole-related litigation.

I am aware that records concerning individuals such as Anthony Casso, Alphonse D’Arco, Thomas Carew, and others appear throughout federal litigation and other records relating to Furnari.

I am therefore specifically requesting records concerning such individuals where their statements, debriefings, testimony, reports, information, or other materials were used in connection with Furnari’s federal parole proceedings or litigation concerning those proceedings.

The publicly available federal judicial record establishes that the Government relied upon information from Casso, D’Arco, Carew, and other sources in Furnari’s parole proceedings, and that Furnari challenged the Government’s reliance upon such information and sought underlying FBI 302s and related material.

Among other authorities, see Furnari v. Warden, Allenwood Federal Correctional Institution, 218 F.3d 250 (3d Cir. 2000) and Furnari v. United States Parole Commission, 531 F.3d 241 (3d Cir. 2008).

I am also requesting all EOUSA and United States Attorney’s Office records that are referred to, relied upon, incorporated, supplied, obtained, or otherwise used by the Government or Furnari in federal litigation involving Furnari’s parole matters. The authorities identified in this request are not intended to constitute an exhaustive list of such litigation.

This includes, without limitation:

* all witness debriefings;
* all FBI or other investigative reports;
* all FBI 302s and related interview materials;
* all interview reports and memoranda;
* all statements or summaries attributed to Casso, D’Arco, Carew, or other witnesses or cooperating individuals;
* all information concerning alleged murders, attempted murders, organized-crime activity, or other conduct attributed to Furnari;
* all records used to establish or support the severity of Furnari’s offense for parole purposes;
* all records cited or relied upon in Government submissions opposing or affecting Furnari’s release;
* all records provided to the Parole Commission;
* all records used in preparing Government responses to Furnari’s parole applications or appeals;
* and records used in federal litigation concerning the legality or factual basis of a Parole Commission decision concerning Furnari.

The fact that a record concerns an investigation or another individual does not make it outside the scope of this request where that record was subsequently used, maintained, reviewed, or relied upon in connection with Furnari’s federal parole.

I am not requesting unrelated investigative files concerning Casso, D’Arco, Carew, or other individuals merely because they mention Furnari. The requested material is limited to records having a substantive connection to Furnari’s federal parole proceedings or litigation arising from those proceedings.

Where matters pertaining to Furnari’s parole refer to information originating from Casso, D’Arco, Carew, or other witnesses or cooperating individuals, please include the underlying records maintained by EOUSA or the relevant United States Attorney’s Office to the extent those records were used or relied upon in connection with Furnari’s parole or parole-related litigation.

Where information concerning Furnari, Casso, D’Arco, Carew, or other witnesses or cooperating individuals has already been officially disclosed in federal judicial proceedings, Government filings, publicly filed exhibits, or other official public records, please consider such official disclosures when evaluating the applicability of any claimed exemption or withholding. Where legally permissible, please release the publicly disclosed portions and any reasonably segregable non-exempt portions of responsive records.



Known Publicly Identifiable Proceedings and Search Leads

The following publicly identifiable proceedings, decisions, dates, docket numbers, names, and factual references are provided as search leads and cross-reference points to assist EOUSA and the relevant United States Attorney’s Offices in locating responsive records. They are not intended to limit the scope of this request.

Please search for records associated with or referring to:

* Furnari’s December 1996 initial federal parole hearing and resulting Category Eight offense-severity determination;
* the August 19, 1997 National Appeals Board decision concerning Furnari;
* Furnari’s December 1998 statutory interim parole hearing and related proceedings;
* Furnari v. Warden, Allenwood Federal Correctional Institution, 218 F.3d 250 (3d Cir. 2000);
* proceedings following the Third Circuit’s 2000 decision and any resulting remand;
* subsequent parole reconsideration and reevaluation proceedings, including the 2002 proceedings;
* subsequent federal litigation concerning Furnari’s parole;
* the Third Circuit proceedings reported at 125 Fed. App’x 435 (3d Cir. 2005);
* the June 8, 2005 statutory interim parole hearing;
* the United States Parole Commission’s July 8, 2005 decision;
* the National Appeals Board’s November 23, 2005 decision;
* subsequent federal habeas proceedings concerning the 2005 parole decisions;
* Furnari v. United States Parole Commission, 531 F.3d 241 (3d Cir. 2008), Third Circuit No. 07-2853; 
Furnari v. Warden (4:98-cv-00222)
District Court, M.D. Pennsylvania

Furnari v. USPC (4:02-cv-00555)
District Court, M.D. Pennsylvania

Furnari v. U. S. Parole Commission (4:03-cv-02046)
District Court, M.D. Pennsylvania

Furnari v. United States Parole Commission (3:12-cv-02378)
District Court, M.D. Pennsylvania

Furnari v. Warden (99-3701)
Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit

Furnari v. US Parole Comm (04-2109)
Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit

Furnari v. US Parole Comm (07-2853)
Court of Appeals for the Third Circuit and
* any other federal district court, court of appeals, or related Government proceeding concerning Furnari’s parole, parole eligibility, offense-severity rating, reconsideration date, release, or continued confinement.

Please use these proceedings as cross-reference and search leads, including the names of the parties, docket numbers, dates, attorneys, witnesses, and factual matters identified in those proceedings.

The omission of any proceeding, docket number, citation, witness, document, or factual matter from this list shall not limit the scope of this request.



Records Supporting Parole Denials or Continued Incarceration

I specifically request records reflecting the factual or evidentiary basis for any Government position that Furnari should not be released on parole, should remain incarcerated, should receive a later reconsideration date, or should otherwise be denied or delayed in obtaining release.

This includes records identifying or discussing:

* reasons for opposing release;
* aggravating factors;
* alleged conduct considered relevant to parole;
* information concerning Furnari’s role in organized crime;
* information concerning murders or other violent conduct attributed to Furnari;
* information concerning his offense severity;
* information concerning his conduct while incarcerated, where considered in parole proceedings;
* information concerning prior or subsequent criminal conduct, where considered for parole purposes;
* witness or informant information relied upon against Furnari; and
* any other information used to argue that Furnari should remain incarcerated.



Complete Parole-Related File and Cross-References

Please search for both main-file and cross-reference records relating to Furnari’s federal parole.

Where a parole-related record references another file, investigation, prosecution, witness, document, exhibit, memorandum, or other source that was used in connection with Furnari’s parole, please search for and provide that underlying record to the extent it is maintained by EOUSA or a United States Attorney’s Office and falls within the scope of this request.

The request therefore includes records that may not themselves be titled or indexed as “parole” records but which were incorporated into, relied upon for, maintained in connection with, or otherwise used in Furnari’s parole proceedings.

The publicly available judicial record demonstrates that Furnari’s parole proceedings involved substantial factual and evidentiary material concerning alleged murders, violent conduct, organized-crime activity, and information attributed to witnesses and cooperating individuals, including Anthony Casso and Alphonse D’Arco.

Accordingly, please treat references in judicial opinions, pleadings, exhibits, Government filings, Parole Commission records, and other publicly available materials to particular evidence, witnesses, investigations, reports, factual allegations, or other sources as search leads for the underlying EOUSA records, rather than limiting the search to documents whose titles contain the word “parole.”

In particular, where a Government filing, court submission, Parole Commission submission, memorandum, or other record identifies or relies upon a particular investigative report, witness statement, debriefing, interview, memorandum, FBI report, prosecution file, or other source, please search for the underlying record and any EOUSA/USAO copy, referral, transmittal, summary, or derivative document maintained by the Government.



Records Systems and Archived Holdings

Please conduct a comprehensive search of the EOUSA records and information systems reasonably expected to contain responsive material, including current, legacy, retired, archived, physical, electronic, and off-site records.

This includes, but is not limited to,
• The USA-5 case management system, LIONS, and any retired or legacy databases;
• The Criminal, Civil, Asset Forfeiture, and Appeals divisions’ electronic and paper files;
• The Records Information Management System (RIMS) and any archived boxes or transferred holdings;

* USA-001 – Administrative File System
* USA-003 – Citizen Complaint Files
* USA-005 – Civil Case Files
* USA-006 – Consumer Complaint Files
* USA-007 – Criminal Case Files
* USA-009 – Kline District of Columbia and Maryland Stock and Land Fraud Inter-Relationship Filing System
* USA-010 – Major Crimes Division Investigative Files
* USA-011 – Prosecutor’s Management Information System (PROMIS)
* USA-013 – U.S. Attorney, District of Columbia Superior Court Division, Criminal Files
* USA-014 – Pre-Trial Diversion Program Files

Please cross-reference searches between these systems and the records of the Southern District of New York and Middle District of Pennsylvania, as well as any other United States Attorney’s Office or EOUSA component reasonably likely to possess responsive records.

The search should include Federal Records Center holdings, archived and retired files, and any other location where historical EOUSA or United States Attorney’s Office records may reasonably be maintained.



Records No Longer Maintained

If the approximately two-foot file, or any other responsive Furnari parole records, are no longer maintained by EOUSA or the relevant United States Attorney’s Office, please provide records sufficient to establish their disposition, including any transfer, accession, archival, retirement, or destruction records.

If responsive material was transferred to another DOJ component, Federal Records Center, NARA, or another repository, please identify the receiving location and any available accession, box, file, transfer, or other identifying information.

If any responsive records were destroyed, please provide any existing records identifying the destruction, including applicable disposition authority, retention schedule, destruction date, or other available documentation.



Referrals and Inter-Agency Records

If EOUSA determines that any responsive record originated with another DOJ component or federal agency, please process the record to the fullest extent permitted by FOIA and, where appropriate, refer the record to the originating component for direct FOIA consideration.

Please identify any such referral, the receiving component, and the records or portions of records referred.

This request is intended to obtain EOUSA and United States Attorney’s Office records and copies of records maintained by those offices, including records originally created by another agency but subsequently obtained, maintained, reviewed, relied upon, transmitted, or incorporated by EOUSA or a United States Attorney’s Office in connection with Furnari’s federal parole.



Duplicate Records

Where identical duplicate copies of the same record are located in multiple files or systems, one complete copy is sufficient, provided that the production identifies, where reasonably possible, the different file, system, or location in which the duplicate was located.



Search Methodology and Responsive Systems

To the extent reasonably possible, please identify the records systems, databases, indices, physical files, archival holdings, and other repositories searched in responding to this request, including the relevant EOUSA component and/or United States Attorney’s Office searched.

If a particular repository or records system was not searched because EOUSA determined it was not reasonably likely to contain responsive records, please identify that determination in the response.



Segregability and Withholdings

If any responsive record is withheld in whole or in part, please release all reasonably segregable portions and identify the specific exemption relied upon for each withholding or redaction.

For records withheld in full, please provide an itemized description of the withheld material, including the number of pages or other records withheld, to the extent permitted by law.

Where portions of a responsive record are publicly available or have previously been officially disclosed, please release those portions where legally permissible.



Fees

I am reserving my right for a fee waiver at the moment.


Supporting Documentation

I have included documentation establishing that Christopher Furnari is deceased, his obituary.

I am also providing the United States Parole Commission document specifically referencing the approximately two-foot stack of files associated with Furnari’s case. This document is provided as a search lead and as evidence of the existence of a substantial collection of records associated with the case.

Please treat the attached document as a specific identifying reference for the requested collection, but not as a limitation upon the broader request for all EOUSA and United States Attorney’s Office records pertaining to Furnari’s federal parole.

The requested documents will be made available to the general public, and this request is not being made for commercial purposes.

In the event that there are fees, I would be grateful if you would inform me of the total charges in advance of fulfilling my request. I would prefer the request filled electronically, by e-mail attachment if available or CD-ROM if not.

Thank you in advance for your anticipated cooperation in this matter. I look forward to receiving your response to this request within 20 business days, as the statute requires.

Sincerely,

L. Joe
